# YOLO-Based Adaptive Traffic Intersection Controller

## Overview

This project presents a real-time adaptive traffic signal control system based on computer vision. The system uses YOLO-based object detection to estimate traffic density from live camera feeds and dynamically adjusts traffic signal green durations accordingly. A host computer performs vision processing and decision-making, while an Arduino microcontroller executes signal control based on received timing data.

The system is intended as a prototype and research-oriented implementation for experimentation, evaluation, and further development. It is not designed for deployment in real-world traffic infrastructure without modications.

---

## System Architecture

The system is divided into two primary components: a host computer responsible for perception and control logic, and a microcontroller responsible for physical signal execution.

### Host Computer (Python)

The host computer performs the following functions:

- Captures live video streams from multiple cameras
- Executes YOLO-based object detection on each traffic approach
- Counts detected vehicles per approach
- Computes proportional green-light durations based on traffic density
- Transmits timing data to the microcontroller via serial communication

### Microcontroller (Arduino)

The microcontroller performs the following functions:

- Receives timing data from the host computer
- Controls traffic signal phase sequencing
- Displays system status and countdown timers on an I2C LCD
- Executes traffic phases according to received timing parameters

---

## Key Features

- Multi-camera traffic monitoring (up to four approaches)
- YOLO-based vehicle detection
- Adaptive green-light timing proportional to detected traffic
- Serial communication with millisecond-level timing precision
- I2C LCD status display with startup diagnostics
- Safe fallback behavior when no vehicles are detected
- Fixed upper bound of four traffic approaches

---

## Hardware Requirements

- Arduino Uno, Nano, or Mega
- One to four USB cameras or webcams
- 16×2 I2C LCD (default address: `0x27`)
- USB connection between host computer and Arduino
- Supporting electronics for traffic signal simulation or indication

---

## Software Requirements

### Operating System

- Windows, Linux, or macOS  
  (Windows is recommended when operating multiple USB cameras)

### Python Environment

- Python version 3.8 or newer
- Virtual environment usage is recommended but non necessary

### Python Dependencies

- ultralytics
- opencv-python
- pyserial
- numpy
- torch

---

## GPU Acceleration

The system supports GPU acceleration through CUDA-enabled PyTorch installations. When a compatible NVIDIA GPU is available, YOLO inference automatically executes on the GPU without requiring code changes.

CPU-only execution is supported but results in reduced performance, particularly when processing multiple camera streams.

---

## YOLO Model Requirements

A trained YOLO model file must be provided separately and placed in the project root directory. The model should be capable of detecting traffic-relevant object classes, including but not limited to:

- car
- truck
- bus
- motorbike
- person

The trained model file is not included in this repository.

---

## Arduino Firmware Requirements

The Arduino firmware requires the following:

- Installation of the `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library
- Correct selection of the target board and serial port
- Firmware upload via the Arduino IDE

The firmware uses blocking delays to execute traffic phases and assumes a fixed maximum of four approaches.

---

## System Configuration

System behavior is controlled through configuration values defined in the Python controller script, including:

- Serial port selection for Arduino communication
- Camera index mapping for traffic approaches
- YOLO model file path
- Total traffic cycle duration

Each camera index corresponds to a single traffic approach. The total cycle duration is divided proportionally based on detected vehicle counts.

---

## Runtime Behavior

During execution:

- Live camera feeds are displayed for monitoring
- Vehicle counts are updated continuously
- Green-light timing values are recalculated dynamically
- Timing data is transmitted to the Arduino in real time
- Traffic signal phases adapt based on observed traffic density

---

## Serial Communication Protocol

The host computer transmits traffic timing data to the Arduino using the following format:

<green_ms_1>,<green_ms_2>,...,<green_ms_n>;<active_roads>\n


Example:

5000,3000,8000,4000;4


All timing values are expressed in milliseconds. The order of values corresponds to traffic phase indexing, and the active roads parameter specifies the number of phases to be executed.

---

## Known Limitations

- YOLO inference is serialized across camera streams
- Performance degrades significantly on CPU-only systems
- Arduino firmware relies on blocking delays
- Vehicle detection is count-based only, with no tracking or ID persistence

For aquiring the database, you may use the following website: https://universe.roboflow.com/
the model will need to be trained on any database selected or created. It it recomended to do this on a powerfull computer as it is very cpu and gpu intensive.
